    <item>
      <title>Avi Chawla Coding agent just got a lot more independent: They can now do the one</title>
      <link>https://t.me/realpdot/9462</link>
      <description>&lt;div class="tgme_widget_message_text js-message_text" dir="auto"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Avi Chawla&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Coding agent just got a lot more independent:&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;They can now do the one thing they used to need you for.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;When you ask an agent to build an app, it can write the schema, the routes, and the data layer in a few minutes.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;But then it needs a database, and everything stops since someone has to open a browser, create an account, provision a database, set up a user, and paste the connection string back into the session.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Those steps were built for a human, so the agent just waits for you to complete them.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;MongoDB just solved that with Atlas Ephemeral Clusters.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Now, your agent can make one API call, and Atlas returns a live database in under a minute.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Your agent saves the connection string, allowing you to keep building on a real MongoDB Atlas cluster.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;You can learn more here: &lt;a href="https://t.co/1AvhBezHTV" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;https://t.co/1AvhBezHTV&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The part I like most is that the agent can create the database cluster and work with the data, but only a human can make the decision to claim it and take ownership.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;If you don’t claim it, the cluster pauses after 48 hours and deletes itself after 7 days, so abandoned experiments clean themselves up automatically.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;If you claim it, it becomes a permanent free Atlas cluster with the same data and the same connection string.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;So the agent handles the setup end-to-end, and you only step in once the project is worth keeping.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Atlas Ephemeral Clusters are in public preview, and the docs shared above walk you through the full flow.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The video below shows this in action, and I worked with the MongoDB team to put this together.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;If you are building with coding agents, the next step is getting several of them to work together on one project.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;I wrote a full breakdown of how Claude Code handles that with subagents and agent teams.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;It covers when a subagent is enough, when you need a team of agents that coordinate with each other, and how each setup manages context and hands off work.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Read it below.&lt;code&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;https://t.co/lVnuSmNYo7 &lt;/code&gt;- &lt;a href="https://x.com/_avichawla/status/2050463606719000627" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;Avi Chawla&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;a href="https://x.com/_avichawla/status/2121906286845669586" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;tweet&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description>
      <content:encoded>&lt;div class="tgme_widget_message_text js-message_text" dir="auto"&gt;&lt;b&gt;Avi Chawla&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Coding agent just got a lot more independent:&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;They can now do the one thing they used to need you for.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;When you ask an agent to build an app, it can write the schema, the routes, and the data layer in a few minutes.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;But then it needs a database, and everything stops since someone has to open a browser, create an account, provision a database, set up a user, and paste the connection string back into the session.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Those steps were built for a human, so the agent just waits for you to complete them.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;MongoDB just solved that with Atlas Ephemeral Clusters.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Now, your agent can make one API call, and Atlas returns a live database in under a minute.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Your agent saves the connection string, allowing you to keep building on a real MongoDB Atlas cluster.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;You can learn more here: &lt;a href="https://t.co/1AvhBezHTV" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;https://t.co/1AvhBezHTV&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The part I like most is that the agent can create the database cluster and work with the data, but only a human can make the decision to claim it and take ownership.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;If you don’t claim it, the cluster pauses after 48 hours and deletes itself after 7 days, so abandoned experiments clean themselves up automatically.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;If you claim it, it becomes a permanent free Atlas cluster with the same data and the same connection string.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;So the agent handles the setup end-to-end, and you only step in once the project is worth keeping.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Atlas Ephemeral Clusters are in public preview, and the docs shared above walk you through the full flow.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;The video below shows this in action, and I worked with the MongoDB team to put this together.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;If you are building with coding agents, the next step is getting several of them to work together on one project.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;I wrote a full breakdown of how Claude Code handles that with subagents and agent teams.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;It covers when a subagent is enough, when you need a team of agents that coordinate with each other, and how each setup manages context and hands off work.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Read it below.&lt;code&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;https://t.co/lVnuSmNYo7 &lt;/code&gt;- &lt;a href="https://x.com/_avichawla/status/2050463606719000627" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;Avi Chawla&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;a href="https://x.com/_avichawla/status/2121906286845669586" onclick="return confirm('Open this link?\n\n'+this.href);" rel="noopener" target="_blank"&gt;tweet&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</content:encoded>
